1. postTweet()

Code:
posts.push_back();

You insert a tweet at the end of the vector.
- Time Complexity: O(1) amortized.
- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1) per operation.
2. getNewsFeed()

You iterate backwards through the posts vector:

for(int i = posts.size() - 1; i >= 0 && n < 10; i--)

In the worst case, you may traverse all T tweets if fewer than 10 relevant tweets exist.

For each tweet, you check:

friends[userId].count(posts[i].first)

Since friends uses an unordered_map and unordered_set, lookup takes O(1) average time.
- Time Complexity: O(T) average.
- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1), excluding the returned answer.
The answer vector stores at most 10 tweets, so its space is O(1).

